y for  rbh might be welcomed by many but the  death of a wab ishak has raised a number  of claims of wider failings in an  organization designed to protect and to  support people in need the culture was  horrible it was bullying toxic the  amount of people I know who left the  organization were in tears for six years  Dave not his real name worked at rbh  before resigning he asked us to protect  his identity as he spoke of his  experience at the Housing Association  the total disregard for the tenants and  the cost cutting was completely obvious  and it's really sad to be honest I've  seen tenants suffering because of this  culture  awab's family felt discriminated against  because of they say what they look like  and where they're from Rochdale Bureau  housing we have a message for you  stop discriminating stop being racist  stop providing unfair treatment to  people coming from abroad to a refugees  or Asylum Seekers an allegation Dave  agrees with is rbh a racist organization  in your view in my view yeah yeah I  think it's sad to say but it is rbh  accept assumptions were wrongly made in  awab's case but said they were not a  racist organization they told us their  culture is one of collaboration and  mutuality Dave also believes there's  only one way to avoid another tragedy do  you think any more people could die at  the hands of rbh's properties I  definitely think there'll be more deaths  due to lack of repairs the only way that  tenants will be safe is if rbh is closed  down and the council takes it back over  we may never know exactly what went on  inside rbh in the days and weeks leading  up to awab's death but it's clear that  for all the unanswered questions it's  more than just the office culture that's  in need of repair  into man Rashid Sky News Rochdale
 

